# C API Design Rules

Apply these rules when writing or reviewing any public C API header or implementation.

---

## Naming

- One prefix per module: `gmf_`, `audio_`, `esp_`, etc.
- Verb–object style: `pipeline_create()`, `buffer_alloc()`, `stream_start()`
- Opaque handle types: prefer forward-declared struct pointers (e.g. `typedef struct module *module_handle_t;`) instead of `void *`; never expose internal struct layout in public headers
- Types: `_t` suffix; constants/enums: `MODULE_ERR_XXX` prefix

## Function Signatures

- Parameter order: context/config → input buffers → output buffers → optional params last
- Use a config struct when there are more than 3–4 arguments
- `const` on all input-only pointers
- Hide internal struct layout in `.c` or private header; forward-declare only in public header

## Resource and Lifecycle

- Every `*_create()` / `*_open()` has a matching `*_destroy()` / `*_close()`
- Document ownership explicitly: who allocates, who owns, who frees
- After `*_destroy(handle)`, handle is invalid — document this; caller must not reuse it
- All error paths must release every resource acquired so far (no partial leaks)
- Document cleanup order when multiple resources are created in sequence

## Error Semantics

- One integral error type per module (`int` or `module_err_t`); no mixed conventions
- No silent failure — always return an error code or use an out-parameter
- Document which error code applies to each precondition violation
- On error, specify whether resources are partially created and who cleans up

## Thread-Safety Contract

- State per API (or API group): thread-safe / not thread-safe / safe with different handles
- No hidden shared state; prefer per-handle state
- Returned pointers to internal data must specify validity window (e.g. "valid until next call")
- Document any APIs that must not be called concurrently

## Configuration and Defaults

- Config structs must have a `get_defaults()` helper or documented zero-init defaults
- Document compile-time vs runtime configuration separately
- Validate config at `create`/`open`; return `ERR_INVALID_ARG` for out-of-range values

## ABI Stability

- Add `uint32_t reserved[N]` or `void *reserved` and a `version` / `struct_size` field to config structs
- Prefer function pointers or feature flags over many near-identical functions
- Never add fields to a public struct without bumping version

## Embedded Constraints

- **Stack:** avoid large local buffers in API entry points; document if stack use is significant
- **Heap:** document whether the API allocates ("may allocate") or requires caller-provided buffer
- **Blocking:** document any blocking call and its timeout behavior
- **ISR:** explicitly list ISR-safe APIs; all others are assumed non-ISR-safe
- **Flash:** avoid macro-heavy or large inline APIs that increase code size

## Per-API Doxygen Comment (required for all public APIs)

```c
/**
 * @brief  One-line summary of what this function does
 *
 * @note   Any important constraint or precondition.
 *
 * @param[in]   handle   Opaque handle returned by module_create()
 * @param[in]   config   Pointer to configuration struct; caller retains ownership
 * @param[out]  result   Populated on success; undefined on error
 *
 * @return
 *       - MODULE_ERR_OK           On success
 *       - MODULE_ERR_INVALID_ARG  handle or config is NULL
 *       - MODULE_ERR_NO_MEM       Internal allocation failed
 */
module_err_t module_do_something(module_handle_t handle,
                                 const module_config_t *config,
                                 module_result_t *result);
```

## Example API Shape

```c
module_handle_t module_create(const module_config_t *config);
int             module_start(module_handle_t handle);
int             module_stop(module_handle_t handle);
void            module_destroy(module_handle_t handle);
```

---

## API Review Checklist

Before merging any public API:

- [ ] Consistent naming prefix and verb–object style
- [ ] Opaque types; no internal struct layout in public header
- [ ] `create`/`destroy` or `open`/`close` pairs; ownership documented
- [ ] Unified error codes; no silent failures
- [ ] Thread-safety contract documented per API or group
- [ ] Config structs have `version`/`reserved` for ABI evolution
- [ ] Preconditions and return codes documented in Doxygen
- [ ] Embedded constraints (stack / heap / blocking / ISR) addressed
